# Preface

// This file contains a formalisation of Nova's type theory: Extensional Computational Type Theory.
// The presentation is by inference rules over a first-order grammar, and
// its OFFICIAL reading is COMPUTATIONAL, in the sense of NuPRL and
// Allen: the grammar is a prior universe of raw terms, a type is a
// PARTIAL EQUIVALENCE RELATION on them, and the one judgement of the
// theory is the equation Γ ⊦ a₀ ≐ a₁ : A — "a₀ and a₁ are related in
// A". Membership is the diagonal: Γ ⊦ a : A is notation for
// Γ ⊦ a ≐ a : A, a term being an element of a type exactly when it is
// related to itself. The setoid model below is this reading made
// precise; canonicity-style consequences are stated as
// meta-properties where they are used.
//
// THERE IS NO TYPE JUDGEMENT. A type is an element of a UNIVERSE, and
// an equation of types is an equation of elements there —
// Γ ⊦ A₀ ≐ A₁ : 𝕌ℓ, with Γ ⊦ A : 𝕌ℓ its diagonal — and types and
// elements are ONE term sort. The universes form a CUMULATIVE TOWER
// 𝕌₀ : 𝕌₁ : 𝕌₂ : … (ℓ a natural-number literal), with the universe of
// propositions Ω below its bottom: Ω : 𝕌₁, and every proposition a 𝕌₀
// code (see THE TOWER in the type rules). There is no top: every type
// is an element of some universe, and "A is a type" everywhere below
// MEANS Γ ⊦ A : 𝕌ℓ for some ℓ. The type slot of the equality
// proposition admits the universes, so type-equality props
// (A ≡ B ∈ 𝕌ℓ) exist and type equality is reflection-complete like
// element equality (see code-eq and its SEMANTICS note in the Ω
// block).
//
// The setoid model interprets the judgement as the relation and the
// diagonal as membership, so typing and equality are one relation
// seen twice, never two.
//
// Semantics is assigned by the SETOID MODEL: a type denotes a
// structural CODE together with a proof-irrelevant equivalence relation
// on the code's decoding — type equality is equality of codes, element
// equality is the relation. The code layer interprets the structural
// fragment; the relation layer is where
// quotients, Ω-truth and the QIIT congruences live (see the SEMANTICS
// notes at their rules).
//
// NOTE: well-typed terms need not normalize in an inconsistent context.
// Equality reflection makes typability hypothesis-sensitive: e.g. under
// Γ ∋ h : (A ≡ (A → A) ∈ 𝕌), the equation A ≐ A → A holds (and
// lifts to types by code-lift), so
// self-application types and ω ω is well-formed at A, where ω ≔ λx. x x
// — yet ω ω has no normal form. Normalization is therefore a property of terms
// in consistent contexts only; any procedure that beta-normalizes a
// well-formed term (as the checker does when storing and comparing facts)
// may diverge under inconsistent hypotheses. This is a liveness caveat, not
// a soundness one: a non-terminating normalization never certifies a
// judgement.

# Type Theory

// We have:
//  * Empty type: 𝟘
//  * Unit type: 𝟙
//  * Natural numbers type: ℕ
//  * Dependent product type: (→)
//  * Dependent sum type: (×)
//  * Record type: Rec l̄ Δ — see "Record types"
//  * Non-dependent sum type: (⊎) — DEFINABLE as a QIIT (see
//    SUBSUMPTION in the QIIT notes); retained as a standalone former,
//    like ℕ and (/)
//  * Equality proposition: (≡), Ω-valued and DEFINITIONALLY reflected
//    (see the Ω block)
//  * Quotient type: (/), by an Ω-valued relation
//  * A cumulative tower of predicative universes: 𝕌₀ : 𝕌₁ : 𝕌₂ : … —
//    see THE TOWER
//  * A universe of mere propositions: Ω — impredicative, irrelevant,
//    and a subuniverse of the bottom of the tower (see the Ω block)
//  * Quotient inductive-inductive types (QIITs): a SCHEME, one for
//    each well-formed signature 𝒮 — see "Quotient inductive-inductive
//    types". ℕ, (⊎) and (/) are its special cases, retained for now
//  * Coinductive types: a second SCHEME, dual to QIITs — see
//    "Coinductive types". Every ν 𝔽 is DERIVABLE (SUBSUMPTION in the
//    coinductive notes); the scheme is kept for structural identity
//    and one-step β

# Syntax

// signature identifier
x ::= <signature-entry-identifier>

// signature context (see DEFINITIONAL AND OPEN SIGNATURES)
Σ ::= ε
    | Σ (Γ ⊦ x ≔ t : t)     // definition
    | Σ (Γ ⊦ x : t)         // declaration (a HOLE — no definiens)
    | Σ (Γ ⊦ x ≔ 𝒮 qctx ℓ)  // QIIT signature definition (sig-qctx): what a
                            //   QIIT former carries, as x[σ]

// typing context
Γ, Θ, Ξ ::= ε      // empty typing context
    | Γ ▷ t        // extended typing context

// typing context substitution
σ, τ, ρ ::= ·           // empty substitution
     | σ, t          // extension substitution
     | σ ∘ σ         // composition substitution
     | id            // identity substitution
     | ↑             // weakening substitution

// label — its own syntactic class, disjoint from the signature
// identifiers x. A label is never bound and never substituted for.
l ::= <label>

// label list
l̄ ::= · | l l̄

// term — ONE sort: types and elements. A term is a TYPE when it is
// typed at a universe 𝕌ℓ. DISPLAY CONVENTION: the metavariables T, A,
// B, C range over terms standing in type position, t, a, b, e over
// terms in element position — one grammar, two reading aids.
t, a, b, c, d, e, f, g, h, m, p, q, r, s, u, v, w, x, z, T, A, B, C, D, E, P, Q, R ::= x[σ]   // signature reference (σ NORMAL: sub-norm)
    | ☐ᵢ             // i-th element in the typing context
    | 𝕌ℓ             // the ℓ-th predicative universe
    | Ω              // universe of mere propositions
    | 𝟘              // empty type
    | 𝟙              // unit type
    | ℕ              // naturals
    | t → t          // dependent product (Π)
    | t × t          // dependent sum (Σ)
    | t ⊎ t          // non-dependent sum (disjoint union)
    | t / t          // quotient by an Ω-valued relation
    | λ t            // dependent product type introduction
    | t t            // dependent product type elimination
    | let t t        // let-expression (definiens, body)
    | t , t          // dependent sum type introduction
    | t .π₁          // dependent sum type elimination (1)
    | t .π₂          // dependent sum type elimination (2)
    | Rec l̄ Δ        // record type: the telescope Δ, a label per entry
    | ⟨l̄ ↪ ē⟩        // record: a label list and a spine, in parallel
    | t.l            // record projection at a label
    | inj₁ t         // non-dependent sum type introduction (left)
    | inj₂ t         // non-dependent sum type introduction (right)
    | ⊎-elim t t t   // non-dependent sum type elimination
                     //   (left case, right case, eliminee)
    | t ≡ t ∈ t      // equality PROPOSITION (the Ω block)
    | 𝟘-elim t
    | ()             // the only element of 𝟙
    | Z
    | S t
    | ℕ-elim t t t
    | class t        // quotient type introduction
    | quot-elim t t  // quotient type elimination
    | ∥t∥            // squash: proposition from a type (the Ω block)
    | ⋆              // the canonical proof of a true proposition
    | 𝒮.i ē          // the former at ENTRY i of a QIIT signature 𝒮, at
                     //   the spine ē: the sort 𝕤 at its index spine, or
                     //   the POINT constructor 𝕔, SATURATED (equation
                     //   constructors mint no term — el-qiit-path). 𝒮
                     //   is carried in the term, written out or a
                     //   reference x[σ], so QIIT equality is structural
    | ν 𝔽            // coinductive type at polynomial 𝔽 (carried)
    | out t          // coinductive observation (the ELIMINATOR)
    | corec 𝔽 t t    // corecursor (the INTRODUCTION: polynomial, coalgebra
                     //   body, seed — 𝔽 is CARRIED, like d̄ at 𝒮-elim:
                     //   el-nu-beta consumes map_𝔽, so the redex must be
                     //   self-contained. The carrier is NOT: a term carries
                     //   only what computation reads)

// universe — Ω, or a level of the tower: what a type is typed at.
// Both are terms above; 𝒰 is only the METAVARIABLE for "either", used
// where a notion is stated at both alike — the targets of the
// displayed reading (the QIIT section), and the sorts of the checker
// (docs/NovaKernel.nspec).
𝒰 ::= Ω | 𝕌ℓ | _

// universe level
ℓ ::= <natural number>

// index: of a variable, of a weakening
i, j, k, n, 𝕤, 𝕔 ::= <natural number>   // 𝕤, 𝕔: a sort's, a constructor's POSITION in a signature

// (type) telescope
Δ ::= ε | t ◁ Δ

// (element) spine
ē, θ, ι, δ, ā, d̄, m̄, h̄ ::= · | t, ē
    | 𝒮-elim ē          // QIIT eliminator: THE SECTION of the displayed
                        //   algebra ē over ι(𝒮) (𝒮 CARRIED) — a spine of
                        //   the section telescope, see Elimination

// qiit-context
𝒮, Φ ::= ε | 𝒮 ▷ 𝔄 | x[σ]

// qiit-type
𝔄, 𝔅, 𝔎 ::= U               // the universe of codes
    | El 𝕥                  // decoding of a code 𝕥 : U
    | t ⇛ 𝔄                 // EXTERNAL Π — domain a Nova
                            //   type; binds a NOVA variable
    | El 𝕥 ⇛ 𝔄              // INDUCTIVE Π — binds a ToS
                                //   variable

// qiit-term
𝕥, 𝕦, 𝕧, 𝕝, 𝕣, 𝕖 ::= ⬡ᵢ     // ToS VARIABLE, de Bruijn
    | ⬡(i)                  // Same as above, just another notation
    | 𝕥 t                   // application to a Nova term
    | 𝕥 𝕥                   // application to a ToS term
    | λ 𝕥                   // external abstraction (binds
                            //   a NOVA variable)
    | 𝕥 ≡ 𝕥                 // equation CODE (in U, at the
                            //   sides' common El)

// qiit-sub
ς, τ ::= 𝕚𝕕 | ⇑             // the ToS's OWN substitution
         | ς ∘ ς            //   calculus, mirroring Nova's
         | ς, 𝕥             //   (the lift ς ▷ 𝔄 is derived: qsub-lift)

## Notation

// How the defined (meta-level) notions are written: alternatives added to
// the categories of the syntax. Each notion is defined where it is
// introduced.
Γ |= Γ · Δ | (Γ)
Δ |= Δ[σ] | Δ ▷ t | (Δ) | ⟦𝒮⟧ | ⟦𝒮⟧ | ⟦𝔄⟧ᵗ | ⟦Ψ⟧ | 𝔗ᴺ | ⟦𝒮⟧ᴰ 𝒰̄ | ⟦𝒮 ⊑ 𝒮⟧ᴰ 𝒰̄ | ⟦𝔗⟧ᴰ 𝒰̄ | ⟦𝒮⟧ˢ | ⟦𝒮 ⊑ 𝒮⟧ˢ
σ |= σ ▷ t | ↑ⁱ | ↑Δ | (σ) | σ, ē | ξ | ξ⁻¹ | ξ_Δ | ξᴰ | ξᴰ⁻¹ | υ | υᴰ | ξˢ | ξˢ⁻¹ | σ · Δ
ē |= ē[σ] | (ē) | t.l̄ | ☐(Δ) | ι(𝒮) | ι_𝒮(𝒮) | ē, t | ēᴺ | ēᵀ
l̄ |= (l̄)
ℓ |= ℓ + ℓ | ℓ ⊔ ℓ | (ℓ)
i |= (i) | i + i
t, w |= t[σ] | (t) | Γ‖ᵢ | 𝒰
    | ⟦𝔄⟧ | ⟦𝕥⟧ | ⟦𝔄⟧ᵉ | ⟦𝔄⟧ᴰ 𝒰 t | ⟦𝕥⟧ᴰ 𝒰̄ | ⟦𝔄⟧ˢ t t | ⟦𝕥⟧ˢ
    | t ē
    | ⌊𝔽⌋(t) | map_𝔽 t | map_{𝔽} t | lift_𝔽(t) | lift_{𝔽}(t) | Δ → t | λ Δ. t
𝔽 |= 𝔽[σ] | (𝔽)
𝒮 |= 𝒮[σ] | (𝒮) | 𝒮 · Ψ
Ψ |= Ψ[σ] | Ψ[ς] | (Ψ) | 𝔗ᵀ | 𝒮 ∖ 𝒮
𝔗 |= 𝔗[σ] | 𝔗[ς] | (𝔗) | 𝔄ᵗ
ī ::= · | t, ī | 𝕥, ī       // index spine: external and inductive arguments

// qiit-telescope: ToS entries over a qiit-context, as a telescope over a context
Ψ ::= ε | 𝔄 ◁ Ψ

// qiit-dual: the binders of a qiit-type, external or inductive
𝔗 ::= ε | t ◁ 𝔗 | El 𝕥 ◁ 𝔗
𝒰̄ ::= · | 𝒰, 𝒰̄ | 𝒰̄, 𝒰 | (𝒰̄)
𝒰 |= 𝒰̄‖ᵢ
𝔄 |= 𝔄[σ] | 𝔄[ς] | (𝔄) | 𝒮‖ᵢ | 𝔄ᵉ | 𝔗 ⇛ 𝔄
𝕥 |= 𝕥[σ] | 𝕥[ς] | (𝕥) | 𝕥 ī
ς |= (ς) | ς[σ] | ς ▷ 𝔄
ī |= (ī)

## Judgement forms

// The grammar of the judgements listed in Judgements below.
J ::= Σ sig | Γ ctx | Γ ≐ Γ ctx | Σ Γ ctx
    | σ : Γ ⇒ Γ | σ ≐ σ : Γ ⇒ Γ
    | σ : Γ ⇒ Γ norm | σ ≐ σ : Γ ⇒ Γ norm
    | Γ ⊦ t : t | Γ ⊦ t ≐ t : t | Σ Γ ⊦ t : t | Σ Γ ⊦ t ≐ t : t
    | Γ ⊦ Δ tel | Γ ⊦ Δ ≐ Δ tel | Γ ⊦ Δ tel ℓ | Γ ⊦ Δ ≐ Δ tel ℓ
    | Γ ⊦ ē : Δ | Γ ⊦ ē ≐ ē : Δ
    | (Γ ⊦ x ≔ t : t) ∈ Σ | (Γ ⊦ x : t) ∈ Σ | (Γ ⊦ x ≔ 𝒮 qctx ℓ) ∈ Σ | x ∉ Σ
    | l ∈ l̄ | l ∉ l̄ | l̄ distinct | |l̄| = |Δ| | Γ ⊦ t / l̄ ↪ Δ
    | Γ ; 𝒮 ⊦ 𝔄 qty | Γ ; 𝒮 ⊦ 𝔄 ≐ 𝔄 qty | Γ ; 𝒮 ⊦ 𝔄 qty ℓ
    | Γ ; 𝒮 ⊦ Ψ qtel | Γ ; 𝒮 ⊦ Ψ ≐ Ψ qtel | Γ ; 𝒮 ⊦ Ψ qtel ℓ | Γ ; 𝒮 ⊦ 𝔗 qdual | Γ ; 𝒮 ⊦ 𝔗 ≐ 𝔗 qdual | Γ ; 𝒮 ⊦ ī : 𝔗 | Γ ; 𝒮 ⊦ 𝕥 : 𝔄 | Γ ; 𝒮 ⊦ 𝕥 ≐ 𝕥 : 𝔄 | Γ ⊦ ς : 𝒮 ⇒ 𝒮 | Γ ⊦ ς ≐ ς : 𝒮 ⇒ 𝒮
    | Γ ⊦ 𝒮 qctx | Γ ⊦ 𝒮 ≐ 𝒮 qctx | Γ ⊦ 𝒮 qctx ℓ | Γ ⊦ 𝒮 ⊑ᵢ 𝒮 | Γ ⊦ 𝒮 ⊑ₛ 𝒮 | Σ Γ ⊦ 𝒮 qctx | Σ Γ ⊦ 𝒮 ≐ 𝒮 qctx | Σ Γ ⊦ 𝒮 qctx ℓ
    | Γ ⊦ 𝒰̄ targets 𝒮 | Γ ; 𝒮 ⊦ 𝒰 targets 𝔄 | 𝒰̄‖ᵢ = 𝒰 | Γ ⊦ 𝒰 univ
    | Γ ⊦ 𝔽 poly ℓ | Γ ⊦ 𝔽 ≐ 𝔽 poly ℓ | Γ ⊦ 𝒰 univ | 𝒰 ≼ 𝒰 | 𝒰 ≼ ℓ | 𝒰̄ ≼ ℓ | ℓ ≼ ℓ

# Conventions: how to read the rules

// The rules below are presented COMPACTLY. Each convention comes with its
// mechanical expansion, so the fully explicit form of every rule is
// recoverable without judgement calls.
//
// * AMBIENT SIGNATURE. Every judgement is relative to a signature Σ.
//   The Σ prefix and the premise `Σ sig` are ELIDED everywhere; rules
//   that inspect or extend Σ (the sig rules, the x[σ] rules) name it
//   explicitly. Expansion: prefix every judgement with `Σ ⊦`/`Σ Γ ⊦`
//   as appropriate and add `Σ sig` as the first premise of every rule.
//   This is the one elision of the presentation; nothing else is left
//   unstated.
//
// * NO PRESUPPOSITIONS. A judgement form presupposes NOTHING of its
//   parts: Γ ⊦ a₀ ≐ a₁ : A is a statement whose truth (in the setoid
//   model) and whose derivability each ENTAIL that Γ is a context and A
//   a type — the judgement validates its own type, it does not assume
//   it. Every rule is stated exactly as displayed, and its premises
//   are those from which the well-formedness of every part of its
//   conclusion is DERIVABLE, by the two meta-theorems below (stated
//   after the judgement forms): the PRESUPPOSITION LEMMA (a
//   derivable judgement has derivably well-formed parts) and the
//   INVERSION LEMMA (a derivable type of a former's shape has
//   derivably well-formed components). A premise is therefore omitted
//   only when the lemmas recover it from the others — `Γ ▷ A ⊦ f : B`
//   yields `Γ ctx`, `Γ ⊦ A : 𝕌ℓ` and `Γ ▷ A ⊦ B : 𝕌ℓ′` — and a rule
//   in which a part of the conclusion is mentioned by no premise's
//   judgement states that part's well-formedness explicitly (the
//   substitution rules that extend a context, `sub-ext` and its kin,
//   carry `Γ₁ ⊦ A : 𝕌ℓ`). Eliminator motives, the middle subject of a
//   transitivity and the data of the coercion rules are genuine inputs
//   and always appear. Expansion: none is needed — what a rule shows is
//   what it says.
//
// * GROUPED CONCLUSIONS. A rule with several conclusions below the line
//   abbreviates one rule per conclusion (same premises).
//
// * EQUALITY IS THE JUDGEMENT. The one judgement form of the theory
//   is the typed equation Γ ⊦ a₀ ≐ a₁ : A. Membership is its DIAGONAL,
//   by NOTATION —

//     Γ ⊦ a : A    is    Γ ⊦ a ≐ a : A

//   — so "a is an element of A" means "a is related to itself in A",
//   and el-refl below is not a rule but the notation read backwards.
//   The equality PROPOSITION (a₀ ≡ a₁ ∈ A) is a separate thing, an
//   element of Ω that INTERNALIZES the judgement: el-eq-i takes the
//   judgement to its canonical inhabitant ⋆, el-reflect takes any
//   inhabitant back to the judgement (the Ω block). The remaining
//   equality spellings are judgements given by their defining rules
//   (ctx-ext, sub-extensional, sub-norm-ext, tel-ext, tel-lvl-ext,
//   sp-ext), their diagonals the corresponding well-formedness.
//
//   THE EQUIVALENCE structure is two RULES, stated once here (the
//   third, reflexivity, is the notation) —

Γ ⊦ a : A
--------------- (el-refl)
Γ ⊦ a ≐ a : A

Γ ⊦ a₀ ≐ a₁ : A
----------------- (el-sym)
Γ ⊦ a₁ ≐ a₀ : A

Γ ⊦ a₀ ≐ a₁ : A      Γ ⊦ a₁ ≐ a₂ : A
-------------------------------------- (el-trans) // via a₁
Γ ⊦ a₀ ≐ a₂ : A

//   (transitivity's middle subject a₁, with its typing, is an input).
//   For the other spellings, refl/sym/trans are ADMISSIBLE,
//   pointwise from these. Pointwise equality of
//   constants and atoms (ε ≐ ε ctx, id ≐ id, ↑ ≐ ↑, · ≐ ·,
//   x[σ] ≐ x[σ], ...) are refl instances and are not stated.
//
// * THE DOUBLING CONVENTION. A FORMER rule — a formation, introduction
//   or elimination rule, whose conclusion is displayed as a typing
//   Γ ⊦ F(x̄) : T — is DISPLAYED in its diagonal form and STATES its
//   full form, the congruence: every metavariable of the term sort
//   (and of the substitution, telescope and spine sorts) that occurs
//   in the conclusion's subject is split into a LEFT instance x₀ and a
//   RIGHT instance x₁; each premise Γ′ ⊦ x : T′ becomes the equation
//   Γ′ ⊦ x₀ ≐ x₁ : T′; and every OTHER occurrence — in the type slots
//   of premises and conclusion, in the contexts of the premises (the
//   Γ ▷ A of a binder), in the substitutions that instantiate a type
//   — takes the RIGHT instance (B[id, e₁], Γ ▷ A₁ ⊦ B₀ ≐ B₁; the
//   left instance's premise is then well-typed by the domain equation
//   and coercion). Premises that are not typings of a subject part —
//   side conditions, Σ-membership, level bounds, the carried
//   polynomial 𝔽 or label list l̄ (inert syntax, compared for
//   identity, never doubled), the ToS-zone premises — are unchanged.
//   A carried QIIT signature 𝒮 DOES double: its premise Γ ⊦ 𝒮 qctx
//   becomes the signature equation Γ ⊦ 𝒮₀ ≐ 𝒮₁ qctx (by name or by
//   content — IDENTITY in the QIIT section), its level Γ ⊦ 𝒮 qctx ℓ is
//   asked of BOTH instances, and every other use of 𝒮 in the premises —
//   an entry 𝒮‖ᵢ, an arity, the initial algebra — takes the RIGHT one. The diagonal
//   x₀ = x₁ = x recovers the display. Expansion: mechanical, by this
//   clause. Rules whose conclusion is ALREADY an equation — the
//   computation rules (β), the uniqueness rules (η), the irrelevance,
//   extensionality, lift/restrict, coercion and reflection rules —
//   are stated exactly as displayed and are NOT doubled; their typing
//   premises are diagonals. So the congruence of every former is its
//   own rule read in full. A rule DISPLAYED in full — the type formers
//   ty-pi, ty-sigma, ty-sum, ty-quot and code-eq, the substitution
//   rules (el-sub, the master congruence, among them) and the defining
//   clauses of the pointwise notations — is not displayed a second time
//   in diagonal form: the diagonal is its instance.
//
// * COMPUTATION. A computation rule — a β-rule, a clause of a
//   substitution action or of a reading — concludes an equation ≐ like
//   any other rule; that the kernel reads it left to right is the
//   kernel's business. The syntax is INERT: no definition on raw
//   syntax stands behind any equation.

// * NAMES. Every rule carries a canonical name on its inference line,
//   following one homogeneous scheme — <class>-<former>-<kind> — where
//   the class prefix names the judgement class (ctx, sub, sub-norm,
//   el, tel, sp, poly, univ), EXCEPT that within the element class the
//   prefix records the conclusion's universe: ty- for conclusions at
//   a universe 𝕌ℓ (types), code- for the rules proper to the universe
//   of codes (cumulativity, restriction, the Ω-formers), el- otherwise. (There is no ty
//   judgement class: a type is an element of a universe, and the
//   prefix is a naming convention.)
//   The former names the connective or constructor, and the kind
//   distinguishes introduction (i), elimination (e), computation
//   (beta), uniqueness (eta), congruence (cong), injectivity (inj),
//   substitution action (sub), and coercion (coe). A -cong name
//   denotes the FULL (doubled) form of a former rule that is displayed
//   in diagonal form (THE DOUBLING CONVENTION); a rule displayed in
//   full carries the plain name. These names are the ones docs/NovaKernel.nspec
//   and the sources cite; there are no other synonyms.

# Judgements

// Each form, with what its derivability yields (THE PRESUPPOSITION LEMMA).

Σ sig
Γ₀ ≐ Γ₁ ctx                  // Γ ctx is Γ ≐ Γ ctx
σ₀ ≐ σ₁ : Γ ⇒ Θ              // yields Γ ctx and Θ ctx; σ : Γ ⇒ Θ is σ ≐ σ
σ₀ ≐ σ₁ : Γ ⇒ Θ norm         // yields Γ ctx and Θ ctx
Γ ⊦ a₀ ≐ a₁ : A              // yields Γ ctx and Γ ⊦ A : 𝕌ℓ for some ℓ; Γ ⊦ a : A is a ≐ a
Γ ⊦ Δ₀ ≐ Δ₁ tel              // yields Γ ctx; Γ ⊦ Δ tel is Δ ≐ Δ
Γ ⊦ ē₀ ≐ ē₁ : Δ              // yields Γ ⊦ Δ tel; Γ ⊦ ē : Δ is ē ≐ ē
Γ ⊦ Δ₀ ≐ Δ₁ tel ℓ            // yields Γ ⊦ Δ₀ ≐ Δ₁ tel: pointwise AT 𝕌ℓ; Γ ⊦ Δ tel ℓ is Δ ≐ Δ
l̄ distinct      |l̄| = |Δ|      l ∈ l̄      // META-LEVEL side conditions on label lists (Record types)
Γ ⊦ 𝒮₀ ≐ 𝒮₁ qctx             // yields Γ ctx; Γ ⊦ 𝒮 qctx is 𝒮 ≐ 𝒮
Γ ; 𝒮 ⊦ 𝔄 qty                // yields Γ ⊦ 𝒮 qctx
Γ ; 𝒮 ⊦ 𝕥 : 𝔄                // yields Γ ; 𝒮 ⊦ 𝔄 qty
Γ ⊦ ς : 𝒮₀ ⇒ 𝒮₁              // yields Γ ⊦ 𝒮₀ qctx and Γ ⊦ 𝒮₁ qctx
Γ ⊦ 𝔽 poly ℓ                   // yields Γ ctx (Coinductive types)
Γ ⊦ 𝒮 qctx ℓ      Γ ; 𝒮 ⊦ 𝔄 qty ℓ      Γ ; 𝒮 ⊦ Ψ qtel ℓ      Γ ⊦ 𝔽 poly ℓ      // AT A LEVEL: every Nova type embedded is typed
                                                                                //   at 𝕌ℓ at its position (Levels; Polynomials)
Γ ⊦ 𝒰 univ                  // yields Γ ctx (Universes)
𝒰 ≼ 𝒰′                       // META-DEFINED order on universes (THE ORDER ON UNIVERSES)
𝒰 ≼ ℓ      𝒰̄ ≼ ℓ              // META-DEFINED level bounds on universes (likewise)

## The presupposition lemma

// (meta-theorem, by mutual induction on
// derivations): a derivable judgement has derivably well-formed parts,
// as the notes on the judgement forms say — in particular, if Γ ⊦ a₀ ≐ a₁ : A
// then Γ ctx and Γ ⊦ A : 𝕌ℓ for some ℓ; if Γ₀ ≐ Γ₁ ctx then Γ₀ ctx and
// Γ₁ ctx; if σ₀ ≐ σ₁ : Γ ⇒ Θ then σ₀ and σ₁ are both substitutions
// Γ ⇒ Θ; and so on for every form. Its companion, THE INVERSION LEMMA:
// a derivable type of a former's shape has derivably well-formed
// components at that former's arities — if Γ ⊦ A → B : 𝕌ℓ then
// Γ ⊦ A : 𝕌ℓ and Γ ▷ A ⊦ B : 𝕌ℓ, if Γ ⊦ (a₀ ≡ a₁ ∈ A) : Ω then
// Γ ⊦ a₀ : A and Γ ⊦ a₁ : A, and likewise for ×, ⊎, /, ∥·∥, the QIIT
// sorts and ν; if Γ ⊦ Rec l̄ Δ : 𝕌ℓ then Γ ⊦ Δ tel ℓ, l̄ distinct and
// |l̄| = |Δ| — and, for an EQUATION with a former on either side,
// the same for that side (the left- and right-hand forms of the lemma
// are proved together). The two lemmas are what let a rule omit a
// premise: el-sigma-e₂ concludes at B[id, t .π₁] from t : A × B alone
// because inversion of A × B : 𝕌ℓ gives B; el-reflect concludes
// a₀ ≐ a₁ : A from s : (a₀ ≡ a₁ ∈ A) alone because inversion of the
// proposition gives the sides at A. PROOF SKETCH. Induction on the
// derivation, all forms at once. The former rules pass their
// components through their premises. The delicate cases are the ones
// every extensional theory has: el-reflect, where the equation comes
// from an inhabitant s of a proposition and the parts are read off
// the proposition's own formation (which is part of the derivation of
// s's type, hence of Γ ctx when s is a hypothesis); the coercions
// el-ty-coe / el-eq-ty-coe / el-coe-ctx, where the target's
// well-formedness is the right-hand form of the lemma at the
// coercing equation; el-sym and el-trans, which swap and chain the
// two forms; code-lift / prop-lift and the two restricts, which move
// levels and need the tower's own rules (ty-univ, ty-prop) for the
// universe in the type slot; absurdity (el-zero-e at an equality
// prop), where the components are the prop's formation premises; and
// the theory-of-signatures zone, whose Nova pieces are read off the
// ToS derivation. No case consults the meaning of a term: the lemmas
// are about derivations and hold in every model. The setoid model
// validates the same entailments semantically — a true equation has a
// type for its type slot — which is the reading NO PRESUPPOSITIONS in
// the conventions describes.

// A derivable judgement under Γ ▷ A yields Γ ctx and Γ ⊦ A : 𝕌ℓ by the
// lemma, so a premise stated under Γ ▷ A carries A's well-formedness
// with it (and likewise for iterated extensions). Γ ▷ 𝕌ℓ is
// well-formed (𝕌ℓ : 𝕌ℓ₊₁): a context may bind a variable ranging over
// the types of a level. What no context binds is "all types": there
// is no universe of all universes, and Γ ⊦ 𝕌ℓ : 𝕌ℓ is not derivable
// (Girard) — a universe is typed one level up and nowhere below.

# Rules (sig)

--------- (sig-empty)
ε sig

Σ Γ ⊦ a : A
x ∉ Σ
--------------------- (sig-def)     // A = 𝕌ℓ is the TYPE definition
Σ (Γ ⊦ x ≔ a : A) sig

Σ Γ ⊦ A : 𝕌ℓ
x ∉ Σ
------------------ (sig-decl)
Σ (Γ ⊦ x : A) sig

Σ Γ ctx
x ∉ Σ
------------------- (sig-ty-decl)   // the A = 𝕌ℓ declaration (a TYPE
Σ (Γ ⊦ x : 𝕌ℓ) sig                  //   hole): the sig-decl INSTANCE
                                    //   at Σ Γ ⊦ 𝕌ℓ : 𝕌ℓ₊₁ (ty-univ),
                                    //   displayed under its own name

Σ Γ ⊦ 𝒮 qctx ℓ
x ∉ Σ
-------------------------- (sig-qctx)   // a QIIT SIGNATURE, defined once
Σ (Γ ⊦ x ≔ 𝒮 qctx ℓ) sig                //   over its parameters Γ and
                                        //   recorded with a level ℓ that
                                        //   bounds it (any bound; the
                                        //   record is what a reference
                                        //   reads its level from)

// A signature entry is referred to as x[σ] — a SIGNATURE, not a term:
// what a QIIT former carries in place of the context written out
// (qctx-ref, in the QIIT section). The definiens is itself a
// signature, so it may be a context, or a reference to an earlier
// entry: an alias, or an instance at chosen parameters. The entry has
// NO declaration form — nothing can be typed against a signature whose
// entries are not given — so it is always a definition.

## Definitional and open signatures

// A signature is DEFINITIONAL when
// every entry is a definition (sig-def, sig-qctx). A DECLARATION makes it OPEN:
// a hole — a name with a type and no definiens, whose references
// x[σ] are STUCK (el-sig-decl below; deliberately no -beta). Type
// holes are the A = 𝕌ℓ instances. An assumed EQUATION is a hole at the
// equation's prop — an entry (Γ ⊦ h : (a₀ ≡ a₁ ∈ A)), A = 𝕌ℓ for a
// type equation — whose reference h[σ] yields the instantiated
// equation by el-reflect: an assumed equation is one el-reflect away
// from an ordinary declaration, and needs no entry kind of its own.
// Open signatures are the working states of an elaboration run: the
// declarations of the run's signature ARE its open proof obligations,
// and a run is ACCEPTED only once its signature is definitional.
//
// The canonical-forms semantics below reads over definitional
// signatures only. An open signature denotes the CLASS of its
// definitional REFINEMENTS — the signatures obtained by instantiating
// every declaration (the one admissible principle next); every
// judgement derived over the open signature holds over each
// refinement. The class may be EMPTY (a hole at ∥𝟘∥, or at
// (Z ≡ S Z ∈ ℕ)): judgements under unsatisfiable assumptions
// carry no absolute content — which is why acceptance demands a
// definitional signature and nothing weaker.
//
// The refinement principle is a META-THEOREM (by induction on
// derivations), not a rule of the theory — the workflow it justifies
// is edit-and-rerun, never an in-place signature surgery:
//
// * INSTANTIATION (signature cut). If Σ (Γ ⊦ x : A) Σ' ⊦ J and
//   Σ Γ ⊦ t : A — the filling term lives in the PREFIX preceding the
//   hole — then Σ Σ'[t/x] ⊦ J[t/x], where [t/x] replaces every
//   reference x[σ] by t[σ]. The A = 𝕌ℓ instance covers type
//   declarations, filled by Σ Γ ⊦ T : 𝕌ℓ. The IN-PLACE variant needs
//   no substitution at all:
//   Σ (Γ ⊦ x ≔ t : A) Σ' ⊦ J follows directly, since every rule that
//   applied to the declaration entry has its conclusion re-derivable
//   from the definition entry (el-sig-decl's conclusion is
//   el-sig-var's) and no rule depends on x LACKING a body — the flip
//   only refines, adding the equations x[σ] ≐ t[σ].
//   DISCHARGING an equation obligation is the prop instance: the hole
//   h : (a₀ ≡ a₁ ∈ A) fills with ⋆ exactly when the equation is
//   derivable in its prefix (el-eq-i) — one open-entry kind, one
//   closing move.

# Rules (ctx)

--------- (ctx-empty)
ε ctx

Γ₀ ≐ Γ₁ ctx
Γ₁ ⊦ A₀ ≐ A₁ : 𝕌ℓ
--------------------- (ctx-ext)        // the DEFINING clause of the
Γ₀ ▷ A₀ ≐ Γ₁ ▷ A₁ ctx                  //   pointwise equation
                                       //   (EQUALITY, conventions)

