## Telescopes at a level

// What the record former is formed from (see "Record types").
// Symmetry and transitivity are meta-lemmas, pointwise from el-sym
// and el-trans.

Γ ctx
------------- (tel-lvl-empty)
Γ ⊦ ε tel ℓ

Γ ⊦ A₀ ≐ A₁ : 𝕌ℓ
Γ ▷ A₁ ⊦ Δ₀ ≐ Δ₁ tel ℓ
------------------------------- (tel-lvl-ext)        // defining clause of the
Γ ⊦ A₀ ◁ Δ₀ ≐ A₁ ◁ Δ₁ tel ℓ                          //   pointwise equation

// META-LEMMAS, each entrywise:
//   * (forget) Γ ⊦ Δ₀ ≐ Δ₁ tel ℓ gives Γ ⊦ Δ₀ ≐ Δ₁ tel — tel-ext asks
//     an entry equation at SOME level.
//   * (raise) Γ ⊦ Δ₀ ≐ Δ₁ tel ℓ and ℓ ≼ ℓ′ give Γ ⊦ Δ₀ ≐ Δ₁ tel ℓ′, by
//     code-lift. The contexts of the later entries are unchanged:
//     Γ ▷ A records A, not its level.
//   * (existence) every Γ ⊦ Δ₀ ≐ Δ₁ tel is one at some ℓ — a telescope is
//     finite; take the greatest of the entry equations' levels and raise.
//     On the diagonal: every telescope is a telescope at some level.
//   * (substitution) Γ₁ ⊦ Δ₀ ≐ Δ₁ tel ℓ and σ₀ ≐ σ₁ : Γ₀ ⇒ Γ₁ give
//     Γ₀ ⊦ Δ₀[σ₀] ≐ Δ₁[σ₁] tel ℓ: tel-sub's induction, each entry
//     el-sub at 𝕌ℓ, and 𝕌ℓ[σ] ≐ 𝕌ℓ — levels are literals, a
//     substitution moves none. Likewise tel-coe-ctx and tel-eq-coe-ctx
//     hold at a level.
//   * (descent) Γ ⊦ Δ₀ ≐ Δ₁ tel with Γ ⊦ Δ₀ tel ℓ and Γ ⊦ Δ₁ tel ℓ give
//     Γ ⊦ Δ₀ ≐ Δ₁ tel ℓ: an entry equation at a lower level rises by
//     code-lift, one at a higher level descends by code-restrict, whose
//     two typing premises are the diagonals (the left entry read over the
//     right prefix by coercion).
//
// The level is IN the equation, so nothing here is a side condition and
// the doubling convention applies as it stands: ty-rec's premise read in
// full is the equation at ℓ, and both telescopes at ℓ are its diagonals,
// by symmetry and transitivity. Equal telescopes need NOT be equal at a
// level each has: descent asks both diagonals. Under X : 𝕌₁ and
// h : (X ≡ ℕ ∈ 𝕌₁), X ≐ ℕ as one-entry telescopes at level 1, and ℕ is
// one at level 0, but X is not — a variable has only its declared type —
// so Rec (l) X ≐ Rec (l) ℕ holds at 𝕌₁ and not at 𝕌₀, exactly as
// X → ℕ ≐ ℕ → ℕ does.

